[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"React Hooks: useState, useEffect, and useContext"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React Hooks are functions that let you “hook into” React state and lifecycle features from function components. Hooks don't work inside classes — they let you use React without classes. React provides a few built-in Hooks like `useState`, `useEffect`, and `useContext`. This tutorial will cover these three essential hooks in detail."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"useState Hook"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useState` Hook allows you to add React state to function components. It returns a state variable and a function to update that variable."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use `useState`, you first import it from React. Then, you call it inside your function component, providing the initial value of the state as an argument. `useState` returns an array with two elements: the current state value and a function that lets you update it. By convention, we name the state variable and the update function using array destructuring."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState } from 'react';

function Example() {
  // Declare a new state variable called "count"
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Click me
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `count` is the state variable, initialized to `0`. `setCount` is the function used to update the `count` state. When the button is clicked, `setCount(count + 1)` is called, which updates the `count` state and triggers a re-render of the component."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Using useState with Different Data Types"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"`useState` can be used with any data type, such as numbers, strings, booleans, objects, and arrays."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('John');
  const [age, setAge] = useState(30);
  const [isStudent, setIsStudent] = useState(false);
  const [address, setAddress] = useState({ street: '123 Main St', city: 'Anytown' });
  const [hobbies, setHobbies] = useState(['reading', 'hiking']);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Name: {name}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setName('Jane')}>Change Name</button>
      <p>Age: {age}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setAge(age + 1)}>Increment Age</button>
      <p>Is Student: {isStudent ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setIsStudent(!isStudent)}>Toggle Student</button>
      <p>Address: {address.street}, {address.city}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setAddress({ ...address, city: 'New York' })}>Change City</button>
      <p>Hobbies: {hobbies.join(', ')}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setHobbies([...hobbies, 'coding'])}>Add Hobby</button>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When updating objects or arrays with `useState`, it's important to create a new copy of the object or array using the spread operator (`...`) to ensure that React detects the change and re-renders the component."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"useEffect Hook"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useEffect` Hook lets you perform side effects in function components. Side effects include data fetching, setting up subscriptions, and manually changing the DOM. `useEffect` runs by default after every render of the component."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use `useEffect`, you import it from React and call it inside your function component. `useEffect` accepts two arguments: a function containing the side effect logic and an optional array of dependencies."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Similar to componentDidMount and componentDidUpdate:
  useEffect(() => {
    // Update the document title using the browser API
    document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Click me
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `useEffect` Hook updates the document title every time the `count` state changes. Because no dependency array is provided, the effect runs after every render."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Execution"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"To conditionally execute an effect, you can provide a dependency array as the second argument to `useEffect`. The effect will only run if one of the dependencies has changed since the last render."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [name, setName] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;
  }, [count]); // Only re-run the effect if count changes

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Click me
      </button>
      <input type="text" value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the effect only runs when the `count` state changes. The `name` state can change without triggering the effect."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Cleanup Function"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Some effects require cleanup to avoid memory leaks or unexpected behavior. For example, if you set up a subscription in an effect, you should unsubscribe from it when the component unmounts or before the effect runs again. To do this, you can return a cleanup function from the effect function."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log('Component mounted or count updated');

    // Set up a timer
    const timerId = setInterval(() => {
      console.log('Timer tick');
    }, 1000);

    // Cleanup function
    return () => {
      console.log('Component unmounted or effect re-run');
      clearInterval(timerId); // Clear the timer
    };
  }, [count]);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Click me
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the effect sets up a timer that logs a message every second. The cleanup function clears the timer when the component unmounts or before the effect runs again. This prevents multiple timers from running simultaneously and causing memory leaks."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Running Effects Only Once"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"To run an effect only once when the component mounts, you can provide an empty array `[]` as the dependency array. This tells React that the effect doesn't depend on any values from the component, so it only needs to run once."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Fetch data from an API
    fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(data => setData(data));
  }, []); // Only run once on mount

  if (!data) {
    return <p>Loading...</p>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Data: {data.value}</p>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the effect fetches data from an API when the component mounts. The empty dependency array ensures that the effect only runs once, preventing unnecessary API calls on subsequent renders."},{"order":31,"type":"heading","text":"useContext Hook"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useContext` Hook lets you subscribe to React context without introducing nesting. It accepts a context object (the value returned from `React.createContext`) and returns the current context value for that context. The context value is determined by the `value` prop of the nearest `<MyContext.Provider>` above the calling component in the tree."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, you need to create a context using `React.createContext`. Then, you can use the `useContext` Hook to access the context value in a function component."},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"import React, { createContext, useContext } from 'react';

// Create a Context
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');

function ThemedButton() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);

  return (
    <button style={{ backgroundColor: theme === 'dark' ? 'black' : 'white', color: theme === 'dark' ? 'white' : 'black' }}>
      I am styled by theme context!
    </button>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <ThemedButton />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `ThemeContext` is created with a default value of `'light'`. The `ThemedButton` component uses the `useContext` Hook to access the current theme value from the context. The `App` component provides the `ThemeContext` with a value of `'dark'`, which overrides the default value for the `ThemedButton` component and its children."},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Multiple Contexts"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use multiple contexts in a single component by calling `useContext` multiple times with different context objects."},{"order":39,"type":"code","text":"import React, { createContext, useContext } from 'react';

const ThemeContext = createContext('light');
const UserContext = createContext({ name: 'Guest' });

function Profile() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
  const user = useContext(UserContext);

  return (
    <div style={{ backgroundColor: theme === 'dark' ? 'black' : 'white', color: theme === 'dark' ? 'white' : 'black' }}>
      Hello, {user.name}!
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <UserContext.Provider value={{ name: 'John' }}>
        <Profile />
      </UserContext.Provider>
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `Profile` component uses both `ThemeContext` and `UserContext` to style the component and display the user's name. The `App` component provides values for both contexts."},{"order":41,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"`useState`, `useEffect`, and `useContext` are powerful Hooks that allow you to manage state, perform side effects, and access context values in function components. By understanding and using these Hooks effectively, you can write cleaner, more maintainable, and more reusable React code."}]
